-boot-security:4.1.0` actually is:** it's one of Spring Boot 4.x's new split autoconfigure modules (the \"fat starter → thin starter + dedicated module\" restructuring). Its POM declares direct compile dependencies on `spring-boot`, `spring-security-config`, and `spring-security-web` (both 7.1.0). It contains `SecurityAutoConfiguration`, `UserDetailsServiceAutoConfiguration`, `SecurityProperties`, plus `.web` and `.rsocket` sub-packages — this is the module that gives you the familiar \"default user + generated password + everything requires auth\" behavior.\n\n**Why no extra setup is needed:**\n- No external service (DB, broker, etc.) is involved — it's pure in-JVM Spring context configuration — so no Docker image applies.\n- No environment variable or system property is required to activate the default behavior; overrides like `spring.security.user.name` are ordinary `application.properties` test config, not external environment setup.\n- Official Spring Boot docs show the standard test pattern is just `@SpringBootTest` + `@AutoConfigureMockMvc` + MockMvc assertions, or plain `ApplicationContextRunner` bean-presence tests — nothing exotic.\n- I checked sibling Spring Boot 4.x split modules already in this repo (`spring-boot-web-server`, `spring-boot-servlet`, `spring-boot-health`, `spring-boot-jackson2`, `spring-boot-security-oauth2-resource-server`). None needed Docker or env vars; each just added ordinary `testImplementation` lines (`jakarta.servlet-api`, `spring-test`, `spring-boot-autoconfigure`, `spring-boot-test`, `assertj-core`) directly in their `build.gradle` as part of normal test generation — proving the generation pipeline already handles this class of dependency-discovery on its own, without needing it pre-pinned as deterministic setup.\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\nSpring Boot 4.0 broke up the old monolithic `spring-boot-autoconfigure` + `spring-boot-starter-security` into focused modules like this one. The giveaway that a \"new library\" issue is actually one of these split modules (rather than a truly new third-party library) is the `org.springframework.boot` group ID paired with a name that drops \"starter\" — e.g. `spring-boot-security` vs `spring-boot-starter-security`. The starter artifact itself usually ends up as a `not-for-native-image` shell (see the `spring-boot-starter-security` index.json I found: \"contains only META-INF files\"), while the real classes — and the real reachability-metadata work — live in the non-starter module.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\nDecision:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"action\": \"no_action\",\n  \"summary\": \"Pure in-JVM Spring Security autoconfiguration module (SecurityAutoConfiguration, UserDetailsServiceAutoConfiguration); no external service, env var, or system property is needed. Required extra test dependencies (jakarta.servlet-api, spring-test, spring-boot-test/-autoconfigure, assertj-core) match the standard pattern already used unassisted for sibling Spring Boot 4.x split modules (spring-boot-web-server, spring-boot-servlet, spring-boot-health) in this repo, so the normal generated scaffold is sufficient.\",\n  \"deterministic_setup\": [],\n  \"agent_guidance\": \"\",\n  \"risks\": []\n}\n```","ttft_ms":6691,"type":"result","duration_ms":208576,"uuid":"92d0a2a1-184f-45c1-82dd-4217d997ecd6","ttft_stream_ms":1672,"time_to_request_ms":130,"first_content_frame_ms":1673,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}

